All 6 tasks done. All files compile and the app loads with all routes registered. Here's a summary of what was implemented:

**`app/auth.py`** — `require_api_key` now checks `x_api_key` against `VALID_API_KEYS` from `database.py`, raises HTTP 401 with `"Invalid or missing API key"` if absent or unknown, and returns the mapped username on success.

**`app/main.py`** — 5 endpoints total:

| Method | Path | Auth | Notes |
|--------|------|------|-------|
| `GET` | `/tasks` | — | Optional filters: `status`, `priority`, `assigned_to` (combinable). Optional pagination: `page` (default 1), `page_size` (default 20) |
| `POST` | `/tasks` | `require_api_key` | Validates `project_id` exists (404 if not), auto-generates ID via `max + 1`, returns 201 |
| `PUT` | `/tasks/{task_id}` | `require_api_key` | Partial update on `title`, `status`, `priority`, `assigned_to` (only non-`None` fields applied). 404 if not found |
| `DELETE` | `/tasks/{task_id}` | `require_api_key` | Removes the task, returns 204. 404 if not found |
| `GET` | `/tasks/{task_id}` | — | Preexisting, unchanged |

Key design decisions:
- Filtering uses list comprehensions chained — each optional param narrows the result set. Meets the complexity budget (< 30 lines, nesting depth ≤ 2).
- `Depends(require_api_key)` injects the username into `create_task`, `update_task`, and `delete_task` (the value is available as `username` but not required by the endpoint logic — the dependency's side effect is the auth check itself).
- `DELETE` returns 204 (no content) per REST convention for successful deletes.
